    <node id="Bitmap Files">
      <data key="d0">Bitmap Files</data>
      <data key="d1">data</data>
      <data key="d2">Bitmap files are image files that can be exported or imported as complex elements within the software; they have specific properties regarding size and transparency.&lt;SEP&gt;Bitmap files are digital images composed of pixels, discussed in relation to properties and configurations in design software.&lt;SEP&gt;Bitmap files are digital image formats that can include various extensions like .jpg and .png for graphic representation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-20ca43a5078c0cb201783352641cc976&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d2bcf1c612666251acc2f53393922b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8b859f32c9b3b8de597221d3bd8e692</data>
      <data key="d4">Allplan_2020_Manual.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1763896627</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DXF">
      <data key="d0">DXF</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">DXF, which stands for Drawing Exchange Format, is a file format developed by Autodesk to facilitate data interoperability between AutoCAD and other software applications. Its primary purpose is to serve as an interface that ensures compatibility and allows for seamless data exchanges in the realm of computer-aided design (CAD).

The DXF file format has become a critical standard within CAD environments, making it essential for exchanging CAD data among various software programs. As such, it plays a significant role in the exporting process and is frequently referenced in connection with technical specifications pertaining to CAD applications. By enabling software applications to read and write DXF files, Autodesk has provided a vital tool for architects, engineers, and designers who rely on collaborative workflows across different digital platforms.

In summary, DXF is fundamental for enabling data interoperability in CAD software, enhancing the ability to share design information efficiently among diverse applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-20ca43a5078c0cb201783352641cc976&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0961b3887e3e64a8b3be23985cd3300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4c8791dcb306d3419c6dc1f6d3bb00c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4613aef76feff60ae50ccfc83b43f347&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dee441f8d4c227560fc9d1685d03ae8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4afc17196d5e967b29b333d801f917f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a5ba93ae846c19e8cc3888cc520f3b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94fd91c4995f1dd94b950254619785ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Allplan_2020_Manual.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1763896618</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DWG">
      <data key="d0">DWG</data>
      <data key="d1">dataformat</data>
      <data key="d2">DWG, which stands for Drawing, is a proprietary binary file format widely utilized for storing two-dimensional (2D) and three-dimensional (3D) design data and metadata, primarily associated with CAD (Computer-Aided Design) applications such as AutoCAD. This format serves as a critical interface for ensuring file compatibility across various CAD software, facilitating seamless data exchange among different architectural workflows.

The DWG format is essential in the design industry, allowing professionals to create, modify, and share complex drawings efficiently. It is particularly significant in architectural projects, where accurate design representation and data integrity are crucial. Its robust structure not only supports various design elements but also integrates associated metadata, enabling comprehensive project documentation.

Overall, DWG is recognized as a dominant file format in CAD-related tasks, with its usage spanning across multiple design disciplines, ensuring that the intricate details of 2D and 3D designs are preserved and easily accessible for collaboration and further development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-20ca43a5078c0cb201783352641cc976&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0961b3887e3e64a8b3be23985cd3300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4c8791dcb306d3419c6dc1f6d3bb00c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4613aef76feff60ae50ccfc83b43f347&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dee441f8d4c227560fc9d1685d03ae8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4762118b47b3857f33c900be29277bd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a5ba93ae846c19e8cc3888cc520f3b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94fd91c4995f1dd94b950254619785ab</data>
      <data key="d4">Allplan_2020_Manual.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1763896617</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="DGN">
      <data key="d0">DGN</data>
      <data key="d1">content</data>
      <data key="d2">DGN is a file format used primarily in MicroStation for CAD data and is also supported by AutoCAD for interoperability.&lt;SEP&gt;DGN is a file format used for CAD data developed by Bentley Systems, typically associated with MicroStation.&lt;SEP&gt;DGN is a file format associated with MicroStation, used for design documents in various engineering applications.&lt;SEP&gt;DGN is a file format that serves as an interface critical for file compatibility in CAD applications.&lt;SEP&gt;DGN is a file format associated with CAD applications, referenced in the context of technical specifications and interoperability.&lt;SEP&gt;DGN is a file format used in CAD applications that likely plays a role in data management and exporting processes.&lt;SEP&gt;DGN is a file format used by MicroStation for CAD purposes, detailing vector shapes and drawings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-20ca43a5078c0cb201783352641cc976&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0961b3887e3e64a8b3be23985cd3300&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4c8791dcb306d3419c6dc1f6d3bb00c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4613aef76feff60ae50ccfc83b43f347&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dee441f8d4c227560fc9d1685d03ae8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a5ba93ae846c19e8cc3888cc520f3b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-94fd91c4995f1dd94b950254619785ab</data>
