    <node id="IFC Object Types">
      <data key="d0">IFC Object Types</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">IFC Object Types refer to various classifications established under the Industry Foundation Classes (IFC) standard, which is significant for Building Information Modeling (BIM). These classifications provide a standardized approach for organizing and sharing data related to architectural design, allowing for effective representation of different elements and their attributes within BIM environments.

In the context of data transfer mechanisms, IFC Object Types play a crucial role in facilitating communication between different software applications. This standardization is essential for managing numerous data structures associated with building projects, ensuring consistency and interoperability among various tools used in architectural practices. 

Furthermore, IFC Object Types are specifically discussed concerning their functionalities in software like Allplan, showcasing their importance in practical applications within the industry. They serve as key components for structuring data within Building Information Modeling, thereby enabling seamless integration and exchange of information among various architectural and engineering software platforms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28f5266d1ec6c127667f218d268add52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5117f1e6eac96242647fb5ad1791c1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f28858a62c0a7e051c948c0bad73c62b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95b3b8fbf6daedada04563720cd956a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce6441641f753eca9fe8ab580e1f82b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0202d05f357226b16b746554c9e7e72d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dcbeb7495b4b6f61c385c7cb39d87f50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ff1ae257d48fb7934b839e7c4b3db2</data>
      <data key="d4">Allplan_2020_Manual.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1763896661</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
