    <node id="Content Fragment">
      <data key="d0">Content Fragment</data>
      <data key="d1">other</data>
      <data key="d2">The Content Fragment is a term referring to specific excerpts of text that have been identified as lacking context, coherence, and meaningful information. These fragments are labeled as 'discarded' due to their failure to contribute substantially to user understanding or guidance. The descriptions indicate various instances of these fragments, highlighting their status as either incomplete elements or irrelevant pieces of information.

One example includes a fragment that consists of placeholder text, such as '37', which conveys no substantial content. Similarly, another fragment features the identifier '75', potentially referencing a page number or list item, yet it remains devoid of significant information. The identifier '171' is also mentioned as an incomplete text element, further underscoring the fragment's lack of context.

Additionally, fragments drawn from user interface manuals, which are deemed insufficient for meaningful interpretation, reinforce the idea that these Content Fragments do not serve a useful purpose in aiding comprehension. Fragments noted to include the word 'Or:' suggest an alternative option within a procedural context but lack sufficient elaboration on the guidelines or instructions related to specific tasks, such as architectural building structure usage or print preview features.

Overall, the Content Fragment encompasses a recurring theme of inadequacy in delivering clear and coherent information, with many examples cited that illustrate their discarded status across various documents and contexts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f586273ea6a7044dc7604e4df904101&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9add4b1a1710511eb95f28026e2db24&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-10c213bf3e73dbb3668162f050af15d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3f3d7ad26c64b09cb8f9bc1bd8ae67a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b04ca9224a2ed064f7912d9fbb3d6def&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a4cf5f011c03d3f7d318f9499a6edd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54141470321b9cf8611ae22bd386ca25&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1aece73f9e99cbab085a302da350f44&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfeef1211e27b8db1beb8722ad8e6b64&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ab331f7587826fc2f982efa84c7252e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26a7bbc5761f5a9b21586b231d0e0df9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7057a10ae54cbc0db0cd954f0d5fdda3</data>
      <data key="d4">Allplan_2020_Manual.pdf</data>
      <data key="d5">1763896702</data>
      <data key="d6" />
    </node>
    <node id="Mouse Actions">
      <data key="d0">Mouse Actions</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mouse Actions involve user inputs through the mouse that control interactions within software applications, affecting the operation and response of design elements.&lt;SEP&gt;Mouse actions encompass activities such as 'Right Click' and 'Click a toolbar' that enable user interactivity with design tools and settings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-abc4489156f7338736e4a38b8b10cc98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d66b0c1cc0e3a04b3d3716ebb8c3e26</data>
